<!-- Added by Plutext Pty Ltd for the docx4j FO renderer (docx4j-fo-renderer), a modified distribution derived
     from Apache FOP 2.11: hook obstacles-in-the-line, fox:wrap on an absolutely positioned fo:block-container
     (fop/CR-024 phase A). -->
<testcase>
  <info>
    <p>
      fox:wrap on an absolutely positioned fo:block-container: the lines of the page it lands on wrap around it.
      A4, 72pt margins (the column 451pt wide, its top at 72pt), 16pt lines; each container is written as a word
      processor's anchored object is, a fixed container in a zero-height wrapper at the head of its anchor
      paragraph, 150 x 100pt at page y 136, four lines down. 1: at the right margin: the first four lines full
      width, the seven lines from 64 to 176 (the first legal break after which a line lies below the foot at 164)
      301pt wide, the rest full. 2: in the middle of the column (room 150 to 300) with the lines on the left only:
      150pt wide. 3: at the left margin, largest side: the lines 301pt wide and shifted 150pt. 4: top-and-bottom:
      no line beside it, the lines from 64 set below its foot. 5: as 1 with distances 12 18 24 6: the band is 124
      to 254, so the lines from 112 to 256 are beside it, 277pt wide. 6: an absolute container 32pt below the top
      of its paragraph, the second: the band begins at 48 in the flow, inside the paragraph's first line, so its
      lines from 32 are beside it. 7: a control without fox:wrap: every line full width. 8: the band begins above the box
      that holds the container (page y 150, the anchor the seventh paragraph): the page's elements are read again
      from its start with the band known, so the one-line paragraph before the anchor whose text the band crosses is beside it too. 9: the
      band covers the top of page 2 (page y 72), the anchor mid-page: page 1 ends where it did, and page 2's lines are
      beside it from its first. 10: a band that the fourth line crosses by its leading only: Word narrows a line whose
      text crosses the band's top, not one whose leading does, and at the foot a line whose box, with the leading
      above the text, crosses it (its readings of 2026-10-15), so the fourth line keeps the full width and the
      eleventh is beside the container. 11: a band beginning inside a paragraph's second line, where orphans keep the
      boundary: the edge is made there all the same (Word's probe cases 3 to 6 and 9, 2026-10-15). 12: a container
      as wide as the column: the lines go below it, as for top-and-bottom (Word's probe case 7).
    </p>
  </info>
